  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nEarly this morning, widespread frost is occurring across much \nof the Northland away from Lake Superior, as clear skies and \nlight winds have allowed temperatures to drop into the low 30s \nand even a few upper 20s as of 2 AM. A Frost Advisory remains in\neffect until 8 AM. Stratus has formed in the upslope flow off \nLake Superior, so far affecting mainly southern Lake and east- \ncentral St. Louis counties. This area of stratus is slowly \nexpanding, and where it spreads, will stall the radiational \ncooling. Otherwise, we are seeing warmer temperatures near both \nLake Superior and some of our inland lakes. After the chilly \nstart this morning, we should be able to burn off the stratus \nfairly early, and then we should see plenty of sunshine for the \nrest of the day. High temperatures should rise into the upper \n50s near the lake to the low to mid 60s further inland. Easterly\nwinds will become gusty this afternoon, particularly near Lake \nSuperior where gusts of 15 to 25 mph are expected. \n\nTonight will not be as cool as this morning- the ridge of high \npressure will slide east during the day today, allowing return \nflow to set up tonight. We are still expecting overnight lows in\nthe mid 30s to low 40s, leading to another threat of frost, \nmainly for inland areas. During the day on Wednesday Southerly \nreturn flow will strengthen on the backside of the departing \nhigh pressure system, allowing afternoon temperatures to climb \ninto the mid to upper 60s across the region, though we can still\nexpect upper 50s along the North Shore. Clouds will gradually \nincrease through the day ahead of an approaching upper level \ntrough. Winds will shift to the southeast and become a bit \nbreezier by the afternoon.\n\nPrecipitation chances finally return to the area Wednesday \nnight into Thursday as a potent shortwave moves across the area \nfrom the southwest. Showers will spread from west to east across\nthe Northland, mainly during the overnight hours, and continue \nthrough Thursday. The atmosphere remains quite dry in the lower \nlevels, which may limit overall rainfall amounts, but a wetting \nrain of at least a tenth of an inch is possible, mainly for \nnorthern Minnesota. By Thursday night, the shower activity will \nexit, leaving us with mostly cloudy skies overnight. \n\nFriday we will still be in that southerly return flow, which \nshould help boost our temperatures into the 60s to near 70 \nnearly everywhere. We will likely still be under mostly cloudy \nskies, but no rain is currently expected. \n\nLooking ahead to the upcoming weekend, temperatures will remain\nin the near to above normal category, with upper level ridging \nin place over the region. Highs by Sunday and Monday should once\nagain reach the mid 60s to lower 70s, with the warmest \ntemperatures across northwest Wisconsin. The forecast does \nremain a bit unsettled as additional weak disturbances move \nacross the region. This will result in off and on chances for \nprecipitation Saturday through Monday, though no significant \nwidespread heavy rain or severe weather is anticipated at this \ntime.",
